{"D:\\wt\\skhpapers\\vlog\\skhpapers\\voz\\b002_t3.wav": {"text": "In Japan we have a word for this work, Seizen Seiri. It means putting your things in order while you are still alive. It is not a sad word. It is a kind word. It means you love the people who will come after you enough to do the hard part yourself. My mother, Fumiko, lived in the same house in Osaka for 62 years. It had tatami mats, paper sliding doors, and a tall wooden chest of drawers we call a tansu, made of polonia wood, with iron handles that clicked when you let them go. When she passed in 2003, my brother Kenji and I opened that tansu. Every drawer was paper. Newspapers folded in four. New Year cards tied with string. Electric bills from the 1960s. Manuals for a rice cooker she had given away before I was married. And under all of it, in the bottom drawer, a brown envelope that said nothing on the front. I almost put it in the bag.", "words": [{"w": " In", "s": 0.0, "e": 0.14}, {"w": " Japan", "s": 0.14, "e": 0.52}, {"w": " we", "s": 0.52, "e": 0.74}, {"w": " have", "s": 0.74, "e": 0.94}, {"w": " a", "s": 0.94, "e": 1.14}, {"w": " word", "s": 1.14, "e": 1.3}, {"w": " for", "s": 1.3, "e": 1.52}, {"w": " this", "s": 1.52, "e": 1.7}, {"w": " work,", "s": 1.7, "e": 2.52}, {"w": " Seizen", "s": 2.52, "e": 3.0}, {"w": " Seiri.", "s": 3.0, "e": 4.0}, {"w": " It", "s": 4.08, "e": 4.22}, {"w": " means", "s": 4.22, "e": 4.5}, {"w": " putting", "s": 4.5, "e": 4.8}, {"w": " your", "s": 4.8, "e": 5.04}, {"w": " things", "s": 5.04, "e": 5.3}, {"w": " in", "s": 5.3, "e": 5.5}, {"w": " order", "s": 5.5, "e": 5.78}, {"w": " while", "s": 5.78, "e": 6.22}, {"w": " you", "s": 6.22, "e": 6.4}, {"w": " are", "s": 6.4, "e": 6.5}, {"w": " still", "s": 6.5, "e": 6.78}, {"w": " alive.", "s": 6.78, "e": 7.56}, {"w": " It", "s": 7.88, "e": 7.98}, {"w": " is", "s": 7.98, "e": 8.16}, {"w": " not", "s": 8.16, "e": 8.36}, {"w": " a", "s": 8.36, "e": 8.5}, {"w": " sad", "s": 8.5, "e": 8.86}, {"w": " word.", "s": 8.86, "e": 9